<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- The private Runtime's Python interpreter on Apple silicon. Every
	     entitlement here must carry evidence that this process needs it; today
	     there is none.

	     Library validation stays on. The interpreter loads only libraries from
	     its own Runtime tree, which the release signs with the same Team
	     before archiving, plus Apple's system frameworks (the Keychain store
	     reaches Security.framework through ctypes). Desktop builds never run
	     pip or uv against this interpreter, and Agent backends and user tools
	     run as separate processes, so no third-party library is ever mapped
	     into it.

	     No executable-memory exception. On arm64, libffi builds ctypes and
	     cffi callbacks (curl_cffi's transfer-progress hook in web fetch) from
	     prepared trampoline pages, never writable executable memory, so both
	     run under the hardened runtime with no entitlement. Intel differs (see
	     runtime-python-x86_64.plist).

	     The fixed identifier bot.avibe.desktop.runtime.python, not the
	     versioned file name, keeps this interpreter's designated requirement
	     and therefore its Keychain item access stable across Python upgrades. -->
</dict>
</plist>
